## How to work here

- `just ci` after each logical change — it is exactly what CI runs: lint, type-check, import
  boundaries, shell lint, file-size and gate-integrity checks, tests, docs links, architecture
  coverage. Don't invent a second linter run; fix what `just ci` reports.
- `just verify` before saying a task is done. It starts as `just ci`; the project extends it with
  what "ran it like a user" means here.
- `just fmt` to auto-format (ruff format + ruff check --fix).
- `just sync` after changing `pyproject.toml`.
- Conventional commits enforced: `feat:`, `fix:`, `docs:`, `chore:`, `refactor:`, `test:`.
- All significant decisions → MADR ADR in `docs/decisions/adrs/` first.
- Coverage ratchet: `fail_under` in `[tool.coverage.report]` (and matching
  `--cov-fail-under` in pytest `addopts`) — only ever increase it. Initial floor is
  stamped from the Copier answer `coverage_fail_under` (per package via
  `python_package_name` as the cov target); raise both values together after measuring.
{% if enable_release_automation -%}
- Releases are automated (release-please, `.github/workflows/release-please.yaml`): a
  Conventional Commit prefix on `main` directly decides the version bump
  (`feat:`→minor, `fix:`→patch, `!`/`BREAKING CHANGE:`→major) — pick it accordingly, not
  just for changelog categorization. Never hand-edit `CHANGELOG.md` or `pyproject.toml`'s
  `version`; merging the bot's standing release PR is what ships a release. See
  CONTRIBUTING.md's "Releasing a version" section.
{% endif -%}

## Architecture documentation

Every top-level module or package directly under `src/{{ python_package_name }}/` needs a
matching `## <Name>` section in `ARCHITECTURE.md`: purpose, public interface, dependencies, and
— if its internal flow isn't obvious from the name — a small ASCII/Unicode box diagram in a
plain (untagged) fenced code block. Hand-drawn, not Mermaid: a diagram you redraw in the same PR
as the code change, not a separate rendering step.

`ARCHITECTURE.md` carries two diagrams above the per-component sections: the **System
Overview** (runtime view — data/control flow, external services included) and the **Component
Diagram** (static view — one box per top-level component, an arrow wherever one imports
another; it should agree with `.importlinter`). Redraw the Component Diagram in the same PR that
adds, removes or re-wires a component.

- `just ci-arch` blocks CI if a component has no section, or no box in the Component Diagram —
  mechanical presence only.
- It does **not** verify the content still matches the code. Run `/arch-review`
  (`.agents/skills/arch-review/SKILL.md`) periodically, or after a PR that changes a documented
  component's behavior, to catch drift the coverage check can't see.
- Add the component's section and its Component Diagram box in the **same PR** that adds the
  component — don't defer it.

## Agent-memory convention

After a significant multi-step task, save reusable learnings to `.agents/memory/`. It is
git-tracked and travels with the repo, so treat it as **public**: project lessons only
(decisions, gotchas, conventions), never personal context, preferences, budgets or secrets.

Claude Code's own auto-memory (`~/.claude/projects/<project-slug>/memory/`) is separate and
private to the machine; it is not linked into the repo. It does not survive a remote or cloud
container rebuild, so write anything worth keeping to `.agents/memory/` on purpose.

When you hit friction (a command that needed a retry, a doc or skill step that was wrong, a
gotcha), append an entry to `.agents/memory/papercuts.md`: what you tried, what you expected,
what happened, a suggested fix, and the file or skill involved. Describe the project, never the
operator. `/dev-coach` reads it and clears the entries it has acted on.

One file per insight; `MEMORY.md` index (one line per entry). This prevents knowledge
loss across sessions and feeds the coaching loop (Factor 2/3 of 12-factor-agents).

